## Why you can trust this data

{% if spot.get('checked') -%}
- **Spot-checked against a second source.** Every run re-reads a sample of {{ spot.sample }} random jobs one by one from a second official address at the job board and compares the title and location. Latest run ({{ spot_date }}): {{ spot.matched }} of {{ spot.checked }} matched{% if spot.get('gone') %}, and {{ spot.gone }} had closed in the minutes between the two reads{% endif %}.
{% endif -%}
- **Doubtful values are held back, not guessed.** In the latest run, {{ q.salaries_held_back | num }} salary figures that looked wrong (far outside any normal yearly pay once converted) were left empty instead of published, and {{ invalid | num }} malformed job ads were set aside. If a whole run looks wrong, it is not published at all and yesterday's data stays up until it is fixed.
- **Nothing silently missing.** {{ boards_ok | num }} of {{ boards | num }} company job boards were read in the latest run; a board that could not be read keeps its open jobs until it is read again. A job only counts as closed after it is missing on two days in a row, so one bad day cannot wipe out real openings.
- **Polite collection.** One request per company per day, plus the {{ spot.get('sample', 0) }} spot checks ({{ requests | num }} requests in the latest run), a short pause between requests, no logins, and only the official public job-board feeds that the companies use for their own career pages.
- **Daily runs delivered: {{ delivered | num }} of {{ due | num }}.** The latest run passed all {{ tests | num }} automatic data checks. Missed or held days are counted in public: see the badges above and the [run log](public/runs.jsonl).

## How it works

![{{ pipeline_alt }}](assets/pipeline.svg)

1. **Collect.** Once a day, the pipeline reads the public job board of every tracked company: {{ by_provider.greenhouse | num }} on Greenhouse, {{ by_provider.ashby | num }} on Ashby and {{ by_provider.lever | num }} on Lever.
2. **Check.** Every job is cleaned and tested. Titles become role types (for example "data engineer"), locations become city and state, and salaries become yearly US dollars.
3. **Store.** Each day's raw data is kept, so history (when a job opened, when it closed, when its salary changed) can always be rebuilt.
4. **Deliver.** The clean table, the [dashboard]({{ cfg.dashboard_url }}) and a monthly download are refreshed. A full run takes about {{ minutes | round | int }} minutes.

