## FAQ

**How fresh is the data?**
Every company's job board is read once a day. The latest run was on {{ run_date }}, and daily runs delivered so far: {{ delivered | num }} of {{ due | num }}. The badges at the top always show the last run and any missed days.{% if sched and sched.late %} The run is scheduled for 07:41 UTC, but GitHub's scheduler has started it late: over the last {{ sched.runs }} days, {{ sched.min_h }} to {{ sched.max_h }} hours late (median {{ sched.median_h }} hours), so new data usually arrives in the afternoon UTC. Every one of those days was still delivered.{% endif %}

**What happens when a site changes?**
The daily checks notice it: that day's data is held back instead of published, yesterday's data stays up, and I get an alert. I fix the collector, usually the same day, and the incident goes in the public log above.

**Which formats can I get?**
For a custom feed: Excel, CSV, JSON or Google Sheets, delivered daily or weekly. This project itself publishes CSV summaries and a monthly Parquet file (a compact file format that data tools read quickly).

**Can you track my competitors' sites?**
Yes, if the information is publicly visible without logging in. Email the list of sites to [{{ cfg.contact_email }}](mailto:{{ cfg.contact_email }}). {{ cfg.brand }} checks what can be collected and how often, then sends a free sample.

**Is it legal and polite?**
This project reads only public pages and official public feeds, follows each site's published rate limits, collects no personal data and links back to every original posting. See [Data use and compliance](#data-use-and-compliance).

**How does pricing work?**
Custom feeds get a fixed quote after the free sample, once both sides know exactly what the data looks like. Monitoring plans are listed on [{{ cfg.brand_site }}/pricing]({{ cfg.pricing_url }}).

